            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The cathedral ceiling of your house would appear to have R24 batt insulation. In conjunction with your extensive renovation plans, I have modeled the opportunity to strap the ceiling down another 1.5" and replace the existing insulation with R40 ......... either batt insulation or spray foam - whatever you choose.



Savings: By increasing the ceiling insulation from R24 up to a minimum R40, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

Your house has a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) system that is designed to "breathe" for your house to manage indoor air quality and manage humidity levels.



At the time I visited your house, the HRV was not working at all and you had most of the windows covered with heavy window fabric. Without adequate ventilation circulating in the house (and over the surface of the windows), the moisture in the indoor air (from occupant behavior - bathing, cooking, breathing, plants, etc) is becoming trapped against the interior window surface and condensing and freezing.



All windows require air movement across the interior to warm the surface and allow any condensation that develops on them to evaporate into the room again. Houses that have furnaces will normally place the heat supply ducts in the floor below each window to help move the air up &amp; over them. Your house has a boiler, so no heat supply ducts - it therefore depends on air movement from having the window coverings open for part of the day ...... and operating the HRV whenever humidity gets too high. You'll know it is too high if condensation &amp; ice are forming on the lower interior edge of the windows continuously - even when the window coverings have been open for 4+ hours.



I recommend that you have your HRV cleaned &amp; serviced so that it works again. Ensure the main controller near the dining room is operating &amp; that you use the bathroom "boost" switches after a shower or bath .... to eliminate excess moisture. If you are cooking something in the kitchen that creates alot of humidity, then please use your range hood fan to eliminate the excess.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

As your hot water is provided from a loop from your hydronic heating system boiler, the estimated energy and cost savings of this upgrade are included in the previous section.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with the original 84% efficient gas boiler that also heats your domestic hot water via a flat plate heat exchanger. This boiler has a "power-vented" chimney out the back wall of the house, and there is an "open" 6" combustion air supply pipe that brings in combustion air for the boiler to burn. For your longer term replacement planning of this boiler, I will model an upgrade to a new ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing "combi" gas boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, that will provide both the heat and domestic hot water for your home. This new boiler will be a direct vent unit that will have 2 small pipes going out the back wall and the "open" 6" combustion air supply pipe will be eliminated.



Savings: By upgrading your current "combi" boiler to a new 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing "combi" gas boiler, you could potentially save $ 1,192 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 32.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program







</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and the one in the basement is an older unit that uses a lot of water &amp; it should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Attic Ventilation



Looking at my pictures of your attic, I notice a lot of frost buildup on the interior surface of the roof sheathing and there looks like some discoloration of the wood sheathing &amp; insulation out by the front &amp; back eaves. My exterior pictures of your house indicate there is soffit venting along the front &amp; back, however I'm not sure that I see any roof vents near the top ? I definitely don't see any gable end roof vents at the top of the left &amp; right sides.



All cold attic spaces require adequate ventilation to keep excess moisture buildup vented to the outside, so that moisture damage &amp; mold do not occur. We had identified that there was some air leakage from inside the house going up into the attic (from around the attic hatch) and I mentioned you could seal this off by using some removable caulking (such as Draft Attack"). Before you seal it, I would suggest you have a local contractor investigate that attic space further to ensure adequate ventilation exists:

    - Make sure the soffit vent area is not plugged with insulation

         - some ventilation baffles installed between the rafters will help

    - See if there are any roof vents near the peak (definitely none by the gable ends)



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation System Design



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) - was not working at the time of my visit

        - Kitchen range hood

        - Clothes dryer

        - Bathroom exhaust fan are integrated into the HRV - so also not operating when I was there



Your house has a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) system that is designed to "breathe" for your house to manage indoor air quality and manage humidity levels.



At the time I visited your house, the HRV was not working at all and you had most of the windows covered with heavy window fabric. Without adequate ventilation circulating in the house (and over the surface of the windows), the moisture in the indoor air (from occupant behavior - bathing, cooking, breathing, plants, etc) is becoming trapped against the interior window surface and condensing and freezing.



All windows require air movement across the interior to warm the surface and allow any condensation that develops on them to evaporate into the room again. Houses that have furnaces will normally place the heat supply ducts in the floor below each window to help move the air up &amp; over them. Your house has a boiler, so no heat supply ducts - it therefore depends on air movement from having the window coverings open for part of the day ...... and operating the HRV whenever humidity gets too high. You'll know it is too high if condensation &amp; ice are forming on the lower interior edge of the windows continuously - even when the window coverings have been open for 4+ hours.



I recommend that you have your HRV cleaned &amp; serviced so that it works again. Ensure the main controller near the dining room is operating &amp; that you use the bathroom "boost" switches after a shower or bath .... to eliminate excess moisture. If you are cooking something in the kitchen that creates alot of humidity, then please use your range hood fan to eliminate the excess.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 7.1 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current gas boiler is a side wall "power-vented" unit that is not expected to be at risk of having combustion gases back-drafted into the house at 7.1 Pascals. If you upgrade to a direct-vent "combi" gas boiler (as outlined earlier in this report), the risk of back-drafting will be completely eliminated.



During my assessment of your home, there was both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Inuvik



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.73 per kWh thereafter

Synthetic Natural Gas (Utilidor Piped) - $37.21/Gigajoule

Oil  - $1.46 per litre 

Propane - $0.69 per litre delivered

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $12.60 per bag, $630.00 per tonne bulk delivered



